  "articlePanels": [{"kind":"lede","html":"<p>iAuditor is the market leader. It is not the right tool for every UK firm. The honest reason firms go looking for an <strong>iauditor alternative uk</strong> teams will actually use is rarely price. It is adoption.</p><p>Here is what breaks, what your options are, and the tradeoffs nobody puts in the sales deck.</p>"},{"kind":"takeaways","heading":"The short version","html":"<ul><li>SafetyCulture built iAuditor in 2012. It reached 85,000 organisations across 95 countries. Built for everyone means built for no one in particular.</li><li>The complaint on Capterra, on G2, and from every H&S manager is the same: engineers stopped using it. They fill forms in the van, from memory.</li><li>Per-seat pricing punishes office staff. A <strong>field compliance software uk</strong> buyer with a mixed team pays for every director who checks a report.</li><li>The real <strong>safetyculture alternative uk</strong> question is not features. It is whether engineers complete the inspection on site.</li><li>Quickler runs in WhatsApp. No install, no login. Per firm: £50 to £300 a month.</li><li>iAuditor offers a free plan capped at 5 templates. Free tiers do not scale to real compliance reporting.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"default","eyebrow":"The actual problem","heading":"The friction is the product","html":"<p>SafetyCulture built iAuditor in 2012. It solved a real problem. Paper inspection forms were a disaster, and digital was better. The app spread to 85,000 organisations across 95 countries. That reach is also the problem. A product built for 95 countries and 85,000 organisations is not built for your firm.</p><p>Here is what happens. The engineer arrives on site at 7am. Van to unload. Client watching. Open the app. Then a login. Then a template that is not quite right for this job. The inspection gets done. iAuditor gets filled in the car park on the way out, from memory.</p><p>The record says completed. It was completed in the car park. T